  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Exiting showers and areas of patchy fog early this \nmorning, with dry weather for the remainder of the day. \n\nThe last of the rain showers from the wrap around deformation band \nwill finish exiting north central New York early this morning and \nbe out of the area by sunrise. Precipitation amounts will be limited \nwith a few hundredths to up to a tenth of an inch possible.\n\nOtherwise, lingering low level moisture and low clouds across \nwestern NY and the Genesee Valley will support a few areas of patchy \nfog, as seen in Niagara Falls and across the western Southern Tier. \nWhile fog will dissipate this morning, clouds will persist \nthroughout the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next round of rain showers arrives late Monday \nnight with strong thunderstorms possible across western NY Tuesday.\n\nThe next surface low pressure system will pass east across the Great \nLakes late tonight into Tuesday, supporting a cold front to pass \nsoutheast across the region Tuesday evening. A leading shortwave \ntrough, just ahead of the front, will support another round of rain \nshowers Tuesday morning across the Niagara Frontier east toward the \nNorth Country. With mid-level lapse rates approaching 7C/km and a \npresence of an EML and effective shear of 35 to 40 knot due to a 50 \nknot jet at 700mb, a few thunderstorms can't be ruled out.\n\nDue to the placement of the surface low Tuesday morning lying to the \nnorthwest of the area, the region will remain under a warm air \nadvection regime allowing 850mb temperatures to climb to around 14 \nto 16 decrees Celsius (nearing the climatological 99th percentile \nfor mid-October), which will result in surface temperatures to climb \nto 10 degrees above normal, especially across the Southern Tier. NBM \ncontinues to show Tuesday afternoon highs for locations north of I-\n90 with considerable spread still mainly tied to the timing of \nprecipitation, cloud cover, and the frontal passage. \n\nThis being said, storms will likely remain elevated due to poor low-\nlevel lapse rates, but additional shifts in the low track could \nprovide more limited cloud cover improving boundary layer \ndestabilization and potential for afternoon thunderstorm development \nover the Genesee Valley eastward. SPC has highlighted western NY \nwith a Day 2 Marginal Risk for severe storms.",
